# reading 2 of aquinas-will-necessary.md by claude-opus-5-5
# Objection 1 argues from Augustine's words (S2, S3) to the claim in S1.
# S3's "it must necessarily be" is read as wills -> []saved: necessity once God wills.
# It could also be read as [](wills -> saved), necessity of the whole conditional; I take the first.
logic	s5
atom	wills	God wills that this one be saved
atom	saved	this one is saved
atom	imposes_necessity	the will of God imposes necessity on the things willed
P1	S2	saved → wills	says augustine
P2	S3	wills → □saved	says augustine
P3	S3	wills → □saved
A1	-	(wills → □saved) → imposes_necessity
C	S1	imposes_necessity	from P3 A1
